// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["cmat"] = {"qs": [["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "What is the volume of a cylinder with radius 7 cm and height 10 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", ["440 cm³", "1,540 cm³", "1,440 cm³", "3,080 cm³"], 1, "Volume = πr²h = 22/7 × 49 × 10 = 1,540 cm³. The value 440 is the curved surface area (2πrh), not the volume."], ["Language Comprehension", "The auditor was meticulous, checking every voucher twice.\nChoose the synonym of METICULOUS.", ["Careless", "Painstaking", "Hurried", "Ambitious"], 1, "A meticulous person pays very careful attention to every detail, which is the sense of painstaking. Careless and hurried are opposites."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "In how many distinct ways can the letters of the word LEADER be arranged?", ["120", "720", "360", "180"], 2, "LEADER has 6 letters with E repeated twice. Number of arrangements = 6! ÷ 2! = 720 ÷ 2 = 360. Using 720 ignores the repetition."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "Annual sales of a company (₹ crore): 2019 – 120, 2020 – 150, 2021 – 135, 2022 – 180, 2023 – 198.\nWhat were the average annual sales over the five years?", ["₹150.0 crore", "₹156.6 crore", "₹159.0 crore", "₹162.4 crore"], 1, "Total sales = 120 + 150 + 135 + 180 + 198 = ₹783 crore. Dividing by 5 years gives an average of ₹156.6 crore."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "In the design thinking process (Empathise, Define, Ideate, Prototype, Test), which stage comes immediately after defining the problem?", ["Ideate", "Empathise", "Prototype", "Test"], 0, "After empathising with users and defining the problem, teams ideate by generating many possible solutions, then build prototypes and test them. The process is iterative."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "A 240-metre-long train passes a signal pole in 12 seconds. How long will it take to cross a platform 360 metres long?", ["30 seconds", "18 seconds", "24 seconds", "36 seconds"], 0, "Speed = 240 ÷ 12 = 20 m/s. To cross the platform the train covers its own length plus the platform, 600 m, which takes 600 ÷ 20 = 30 seconds."], ["Language Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nWhen companies first moved to remote work, many managers feared that productivity would collapse. For routine, well-defined tasks, the fear proved largely unfounded: output held steady and employees saved hours of commuting. The harder problems were subtler. New hires, who learn much by watching experienced colleagues, found it difficult to absorb the informal knowledge that never appears in a manual. Spontaneous conversations that spark ideas also became rarer. Firms that succeeded did not simply return everyone to the office; they deliberately created occasions for mentoring and unstructured interaction, whether in person or online.\n\nWhich of the following best states the main idea of the passage?", ["Remote work's real cost was lost informal learning, which firms had to address deliberately", "Remote work sharply reduced the output of employees doing routine and well-defined tasks", "Companies should bring all employees back to the office to restore their productivity", "Saving commuting time is the most important benefit that remote work offers to staff"], 0, "The passage says routine output held steady; the subtler problem was weaker informal learning and fewer spontaneous ideas, which successful firms tackled on purpose. It rejects a simple return to office."], ["Language Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nWhen companies first moved to remote work, many managers feared that productivity would collapse. For routine, well-defined tasks, the fear proved largely unfounded: output held steady and employees saved hours of commuting. The harder problems were subtler. New hires, who learn much by watching experienced colleagues, found it difficult to absorb the informal knowledge that never appears in a manual. Spontaneous conversations that spark ideas also became rarer. Firms that succeeded did not simply return everyone to the office; they deliberately created occasions for mentoring and unstructured interaction, whether in person or online.\n\nAccording to the passage, which group found remote work especially difficult?", ["Senior managers", "New hires", "Employees doing routine tasks", "Workers with long commutes"], 1, "The passage states that new hires, who learn by watching experienced colleagues, struggled to absorb informal knowledge. Routine-task employees, by contrast, kept output steady."], ["Language Comprehension", "Select the best replacement for the underlined part. If no improvement is needed, choose 'No improvement'.\n[If I would have known] about the delay, I would have taken the earlier train.", ["If I knew", "If I would know", "No improvement", "Had I known"], 3, "A third conditional uses the past perfect in the if-clause: 'If I had known' or the inverted 'Had I known'. 'Would have' never appears in the if-clause."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "A can finish a job in 12 days and B in 18 days. They work together for 4 days, after which A leaves. In how many more days will B finish the remaining work?", ["6 days", "9 days", "10 days", "8 days"], 3, "Together they do 1/12 + 1/18 = 5/36 per day, so 20/36 = 5/9 in 4 days. The remaining 4/9 at B's rate of 1/18 per day takes 8 days."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "The phrase 'creative destruction', describing how innovation replaces old industries, is most closely associated with:", ["Peter Drucker", "Joseph Schumpeter", "Adam Smith", "Michael Porter"], 1, "Schumpeter argued that entrepreneurs drive economic change through innovations that destroy existing products and firms. Drucker wrote on systematic innovation, and Porter on competitive strategy."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Among five friends A, B, C, D and E, A is taller than B but shorter than C. D is shorter than B, and E is taller than C. Who is the third tallest?", ["B", "C", "A", "E"], 2, "Combining the clues gives E > C > A > B > D. Counting from the tallest, A is third. C is second, and B is fourth."], ["General Awareness", "What is the SI unit of electrical resistance?", ["Volt", "Ampere", "Ohm", "Watt"], 2, "Resistance is measured in ohms (Ω). By Ohm's law, R = V/I, so one ohm is one volt per ampere. Volt measures potential difference and watt measures power."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Arun is facing east. He turns 90° clockwise, then 180°, and then 90° anticlockwise. Which direction is he facing now?", ["East", "North", "West", "South"], 2, "East turned 90° clockwise is South; turning 180° gives North; turning 90° anticlockwise from North gives West."], ["Logical Reasoning", "The traffic police of a city claim that speed cameras installed in January reduced road accidents by 30% during that year. Which of the following, if true, most weakens this claim?", ["A new metro line opened in the same January and took many cars off the roads", "The cameras were installed mainly on roads with the highest accident rates in the city", "Drivers caught by the cameras had to pay fines that were higher than in earlier years", "Accidents also fell in areas where the speed cameras were operating round the clock"], 0, "An alternative cause that coincides in time, the metro line reducing traffic, could explain the fall in accidents, so the cameras may not be responsible. The other options either fit or support the claim."], ["Language Comprehension", "Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.\nNeither the manager nor his assistants / was present / at the meeting / yesterday.", ["was present", "Neither the manager nor his assistants", "at the meeting", "yesterday"], 0, "With 'neither…nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject. 'Assistants' is plural, so the sentence needs 'were present'."], ["General Awareness", "Which is the largest state of India by area?", ["Madhya Pradesh", "Rajasthan", "Maharashtra", "Uttar Pradesh"], 1, "Rajasthan is the largest state by area, followed by Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra. Uttar Pradesh is the largest by population, not area."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "A 60-litre mixture contains milk and water in the ratio 7 : 5. How much water must be added to make the ratio 1 : 1?", ["5 litres", "10 litres", "12 litres", "15 litres"], 1, "The mixture has 35 litres of milk and 25 litres of water. For equal quantities, water must also be 35 litres, so 10 litres must be added."], ["Language Comprehension", "Her candid feedback, though uncomfortable, helped the team improve.\nWhich word is closest in meaning to CANDID as used here?", ["Secretive", "Cautious", "Cunning", "Frank"], 3, "Candid means honest and straightforward in speech, which is what frank means. Secretive and cunning suggest concealment, the opposite idea."], ["General Awareness", "Deficiency of which vitamin causes scurvy?", ["Vitamin A", "Vitamin D", "Vitamin C", "Vitamin K"], 2, "Scurvy, marked by bleeding gums and weakness, results from a lack of vitamin C (ascorbic acid). Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets, and vitamin A deficiency night blindness."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "A startup has ₹1.8 crore in the bank. Its monthly expenses are ₹45 lakh and monthly revenue is ₹15 lakh. Assuming these stay constant, what is its cash runway?", ["4 months", "8 months", "12 months", "6 months"], 3, "Net burn = ₹45 lakh − ₹15 lakh = ₹30 lakh per month. Runway = ₹180 lakh ÷ ₹30 lakh = 6 months. Using gross burn alone gives the wrong answer of 4 months."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "A restaurant chain keeps its spice-blend recipe confidential among a few staff bound by non-disclosure agreements instead of applying for a patent. The recipe is protected as a:", ["Trademark", "Copyright", "Geographical Indication", "Trade secret"], 3, "A trade secret is valuable confidential information protected by secrecy and contracts. Unlike a patent, it never requires public disclosure and can last indefinitely if kept secret."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Statement: P > Q ≥ R = S < T\nConclusions:\nI. P > S\nII. T > Q", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Either I or II follows"], 0, "From P > Q ≥ R = S we get P > S, so I is true. T and Q are both only linked to S, and both are at least S, so their mutual order cannot be fixed."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "Under the Copyright Act, 1957, copyright in a published literary work generally lasts for:", ["20 years from publication", "the author's lifetime plus 70 years", "10 years, renewable every 10 years", "the author's lifetime plus 60 years"], 3, "In India, copyright in literary, dramatic, musical and artistic works lasts for the author's life plus 60 years. Life plus 70 years is the rule in the EU and USA, not India."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "A car travels from town P to town Q at 40 km/h and returns along the same road at 60 km/h. What is its average speed for the whole journey?", ["50 km/h", "45 km/h", "52 km/h", "48 km/h"], 3, "For equal distances, average speed = 2xy/(x + y) = 2 × 40 × 60 ÷ 100 = 48 km/h. The simple average of 50 km/h is wrong because more time is spent at the slower speed."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "A founder owns 60% of a company's 1,00,000 shares. The company then issues 25,000 new shares to an investor. What is the founder's stake after this issue?", ["45%", "48%", "50%", "60%"], 1, "The founder still holds 60,000 shares, but the total rises to 1,25,000. Stake = 60,000 ÷ 1,25,000 = 48%. This reduction in ownership is called dilution."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "The 'Blue Ocean Strategy' proposed by W. Chan Kim and Renée Mauborgne recommends that firms:", ["create uncontested market space that makes competition irrelevant", "cut prices aggressively to win share from existing competitors", "focus only on the largest and most crowded existing markets", "copy successful rivals quickly and improve on their products"], 0, "Blue oceans are new, uncontested markets created through value innovation; red oceans are crowded markets where firms fight over existing demand, often through price cuts."], ["General Awareness", "Under the RBI's flexible inflation targeting framework, the inflation target is defined in terms of which index?", ["Wholesale Price Index", "GDP deflator", "Consumer Price Index for Industrial Workers", "Consumer Price Index (Combined)"], 3, "Since the framework was formalised in 2016, the target is set using headline CPI (Combined) inflation, which reflects retail prices across rural and urban India, rather than the WPI."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Find the next letter in the series.\nB, E, I, N, ?", ["S", "T", "U", "R"], 1, "Positions are 2, 5, 9, 14: the gaps increase 3, 4, 5, so the next gap is 6, giving position 20, which is T."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "An article has a marked price of ₹2,500. The shopkeeper allows successive discounts of 20% and 10%. What is the selling price?", ["₹1,750", "₹1,800", "₹1,825", "₹1,875"], 1, "After 20% off, the price is ₹2,000; a further 10% off gives ₹1,800. Successive discounts of 20% and 10% equal a single discount of 28%, not 30%."], ["Language Comprehension", "Fill in the blank with the correct preposition.\nShe has been working with this firm ______ 2019.", ["since", "for", "from", "by"], 0, "'Since' is used with a point in time in the present perfect continuous. 'For' is used with a period, such as 'for six years'."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Which one does NOT belong to the group?\nMercury, Venus, Mars, Moon", ["Mercury", "Venus", "Mars", "Moon"], 3, "Mercury, Venus and Mars are planets orbiting the Sun. The Moon is a natural satellite orbiting the Earth, so it is the odd one out."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "Annual sales of a company (₹ crore): 2019 – 120, 2020 – 150, 2021 – 135, 2022 – 180, 2023 – 198.\nIn which year was the percentage growth in sales over the previous year the highest?", ["2022", "2020", "2021", "2023"], 0, "Growth: 2020 = 25%, 2021 = −10%, 2022 = 45/135 ≈ 33.3%, 2023 = 10%. Although 2020 rose by ₹30 crore too, 2022's rise came on a smaller base."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "A sum of money doubles itself in 5 years at compound interest. In how many years will it become 8 times itself at the same rate?", ["20 years", "40 years", "15 years", "10 years"], 2, "Under compounding, the sum multiplies by 2 every 5 years: 2 → 4 → 8 takes three such periods, i.e. 15 years. The answer 40 years would apply to simple interest."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "How many building blocks does the Business Model Canvas developed by Alexander Osterwalder contain?", ["Nine", "Five", "Seven", "Twelve"], 0, "The canvas has nine blocks: customer segments, value propositions, channels, customer relationships, revenue streams, key resources, key activities, key partnerships and cost structure."], ["General Awareness", "The repo rate is the rate at which:", ["banks lend money to their most creditworthy corporate customers", "the RBI lends short-term funds to banks against government securities", "the RBI borrows money from commercial banks by selling securities", "banks pay interest to customers on their savings account deposits"], 1, "Under a repo, banks borrow from the RBI by selling securities with an agreement to repurchase them. When the RBI absorbs funds from banks, that is a reverse repo or SDF operation."], ["Language Comprehension", "Select the most appropriate meaning of the idiom 'a blessing in disguise'.", ["A gift that is given secretly to someone in need", "Something that seems bad at first but turns out well", "A favour that later causes serious trouble", "A hidden talent that nobody has noticed yet"], 1, "A blessing in disguise is an apparent misfortune that later proves beneficial, for example losing a job that leads to a better one."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Six friends A, B, C, D, E and F sit around a circular table facing the centre. A sits opposite D. B is immediately to the right of A. C sits opposite B. E is not adjacent to A. Who sits immediately to the left of A?", ["F", "E", "C", "D"], 0, "B is on A's right and E cannot touch A, so F must be on A's left. Then C opposite B and D opposite A leave E between B and D."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "An enterprise sells low-cost sanitary pads made by rural women's groups and reinvests most of its surplus to expand access in villages. This is best described as:", ["Social entrepreneurship", "Corporate social responsibility", "Franchising", "Lifestyle entrepreneurship"], 0, "Social entrepreneurship uses a business model whose primary aim is social impact, reinvesting surplus towards that mission. CSR is spending by an existing company, not the core business itself."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "Which sequence shows the usual order of startup funding stages?", ["Seed → Pre-seed → Series A → Series B", "Pre-seed → Seed → Series A → Series B", "Series A → Seed → Pre-seed → Series B", "Pre-seed → Series A → Seed → Series B"], 1, "Founders typically raise pre-seed money (often from themselves, friends or angels), then a seed round, followed by institutional Series A and later Series B rounds to scale."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Statements:\nAll pens are books.\nNo book is a table.\nConclusions:\nI. No pen is a table.\nII. Some tables are pens.", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 0, "Every pen lies inside 'books', and books share nothing with tables, so no pen can be a table. Conclusion II directly contradicts I, so it cannot follow."], ["Language Comprehension", "Arrange the sentences into a coherent paragraph.\nP. As a result, even small shops now accept payments through a phone.\nQ. Over the past decade, digital payments in India have grown rapidly.\nR. This growth has been driven largely by the Unified Payments Interface.\nS. UPI allows instant transfers between bank accounts at little or no cost.", ["QSRP", "QRSP", "RQSP", "QRPS"], 1, "Q introduces the topic, R names the driver of growth, S explains what UPI does, and P gives the consequence ('As a result'). So the order is QRSP."], ["Language Comprehension", "Select the most appropriate meaning of the idiom 'to bell the cat'.", ["To raise a false alarm in order to scare people", "To reveal a secret that one promised to keep", "To keep a constant watch over a dishonest person", "To take on a risky task for the benefit of others"], 3, "From the fable of the mice who wanted a bell on the cat but found no volunteer, to bell the cat means to undertake a dangerous job on everyone's behalf."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "A trader sells a watch at a loss of 15%. Had he sold it for ₹360 more, he would have made a profit of 9%. What is the cost price of the watch?", ["₹1,200", "₹1,600", "₹2,400", "₹1,500"], 3, "The gap between −15% and +9% is 24% of the cost price, which equals ₹360. So CP = 360 ÷ 0.24 = ₹1,500."], ["General Awareness", "The Tropic of Cancer does NOT pass through which of these states?", ["Odisha", "Gujarat", "Jharkhand", "Mizoram"], 0, "The Tropic of Cancer crosses Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura and Mizoram. Odisha lies entirely south of it."], ["General Awareness", "'Stagflation' refers to an economic situation in which:", ["high inflation coexists with stagnant growth and high unemployment", "prices fall steadily while output and employment keep rising", "rapid growth leads to full employment and low inflation", "the government balances its budget during a recession"], 0, "Stagflation combines rising prices with weak output and high unemployment, as in many economies in the 1970s. It is hard to tackle because anti-inflation measures can deepen the slowdown."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Find the next term of the series.\n3, 8, 15, 24, 35, ?", ["48", "46", "49", "50"], 0, "The terms are n² − 1 for n = 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, so the next is 7² − 1 = 48. Equivalently, the differences 5, 7, 9, 11 continue with 13."], ["Language Comprehension", "Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.\nHe is senior than me / in the organisation, / but he never / behaves arrogantly.", ["in the organisation,", "but he never", "He is senior than me", "behaves arrogantly."], 2, "Latin comparatives such as senior, junior, superior and prior take 'to', not 'than'. The correct form is 'He is senior to me'."], ["General Awareness", "Which Article of the Constitution of India guarantees the protection of life and personal liberty?", ["Article 14", "Article 19", "Article 21", "Article 32"], 2, "Article 21 states that no person shall be deprived of life or personal liberty except according to procedure established by law. Article 14 deals with equality before law."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Find the missing number.\n7 : 50 :: 9 : ?", ["81", "82", "80", "90"], 1, "The relation is n² + 1: 7² + 1 = 50, so 9² + 1 = 82. Choosing 81 forgets the +1 step."], ["Logical Reasoning", "If 1 January 2025 was a Wednesday, what day of the week was 1 January 2026?", ["Wednesday", "Friday", "Tuesday", "Thursday"], 3, "2025 is not a leap year, so it has 365 days = 52 weeks + 1 odd day. The weekday therefore moves forward by one, from Wednesday to Thursday."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "In a SWOT analysis of a startup, a large competitor announcing entry into the same market would be classified as a:", ["Weakness", "Strength", "Threat", "Opportunity"], 2, "Strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats are external. A new competitor is an external factor that could harm the firm, so it is a threat."], ["General Awareness", "The Goods and Services Tax (GST) was introduced in India through which Constitutional Amendment Act?", ["101st Amendment Act, 2016", "73rd Amendment Act, 1992", "86th Amendment Act, 2002", "103rd Amendment Act, 2019"], 0, "The 101st Amendment Act, 2016 enabled GST and created the GST Council (Article 279A); GST came into force on 1 July 2017. The 73rd concerns panchayats."], ["General Awareness", "Where are the headquarters of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) located?", ["New York", "Washington, D.C.", "Geneva", "Paris"], 1, "The IMF, like the World Bank, is headquartered in Washington, D.C. Geneva hosts the WTO and Paris the OECD, which are common distractors."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "An employee of a large bank develops and launches a new digital lending product within the bank, using its resources. Such behaviour is called:", ["Outsourcing", "Franchising", "Intrapreneurship", "Disintermediation"], 2, "Intrapreneurship means acting like an entrepreneur inside an existing organisation, taking initiative and risk to create new products while using the employer's resources."], ["Language Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nWhen companies first moved to remote work, many managers feared that productivity would collapse. For routine, well-defined tasks, the fear proved largely unfounded: output held steady and employees saved hours of commuting. The harder problems were subtler. New hires, who learn much by watching experienced colleagues, found it difficult to absorb the informal knowledge that never appears in a manual. Spontaneous conversations that spark ideas also became rarer. Firms that succeeded did not simply return everyone to the office; they deliberately created occasions for mentoring and unstructured interaction, whether in person or online.\n\nWith which statement would the author most likely agree?", ["Productivity always falls when employees stop meeting in person", "Formal manuals capture nearly everything a new employee needs", "Online interaction cannot support any kind of mentoring at all", "Returning all staff to the office is not, by itself, a complete solution"], 3, "The author says successful firms 'did not simply return everyone to the office' and created mentoring 'in person or online'. Hence an office return alone is not enough."], ["Logical Reasoning", "In a certain code, TEACHER is written as VGCEJGT. How will STUDENT be written in that code?", ["UVWEGPV", "TUVFGPV", "UWVFGPV", "UVWFGPV"], 3, "Each letter is shifted two places forward: T→V, E→G, A→C and so on. Applying +2 to STUDENT gives U V W F G P V."], ["General Awareness", "The Battle of Plassey, which laid the foundation of British political power in Bengal, was fought in:", ["1757", "1764", "1761", "1857"], 0, "Robert Clive defeated Siraj-ud-Daulah at Plassey in 1757. The Battle of Buxar (1764) later secured the diwani; 1761 was the Third Battle of Panipat."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "In India, registration of an industrial design under the Designs Act, 2000 gives protection for a maximum of:", ["10 years", "20 years", "15 years", "60 years"], 2, "Design registration is valid for 10 years and can be extended once by 5 years, giving 15 years in all. 20 years is the patent term."], ["General Awareness", "What is the maximum strength of the Rajya Sabha permitted by the Constitution?", ["238", "245", "250", "552"], 2, "The Constitution allows up to 250 members: 238 representatives of states and union territories plus 12 nominated by the President. 245 is the present actual strength."], ["General Awareness", "Under the Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR), a scheduled commercial bank must keep a specified share of its net demand and time liabilities:", ["in gold and approved securities with itself", "as a cash balance with the RBI", "as loans to priority-sector borrowers", "as fixed deposits with NABARD"], 1, "CRR is held as cash balance with the RBI and earns no interest. Holding liquid assets such as gold, cash and government securities with the bank itself is the SLR requirement."], ["General Awareness", "Which Article did Dr B. R. Ambedkar describe as the 'heart and soul' of the Constitution?", ["Article 21", "Article 226", "Article 368", "Article 32"], 3, "Article 32, the Right to Constitutional Remedies, lets citizens move the Supreme Court to enforce Fundamental Rights. Article 226 gives similar but wider writ powers to High Courts."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "Two fair dice are thrown together. What is the probability that the sum of the numbers shown is 8?", ["1/6", "1/9", "7/36", "5/36"], 3, "Favourable outcomes are (2,6), (3,5), (4,4), (5,3) and (6,2): five out of 36 equally likely outcomes, so the probability is 5/36."], ["Language Comprehension", "Choose the one word for: a person who does not believe in the existence of God.", ["Atheist", "Agnostic", "Theist", "Heretic"], 0, "An atheist denies the existence of God. An agnostic holds that it cannot be known, a theist believes, and a heretic holds views opposed to accepted doctrine."], ["Logical Reasoning", "What is the angle between the hour hand and the minute hand of a clock at 3:40?", ["120°", "130°", "140°", "150°"], 1, "Angle = |30H − 5.5M| = |90 − 220| = 130°. The hour hand moves too, so it is not simply the 120° between the 4 and 8 marks."], ["General Awareness", "Mahatma Gandhi's Dandi March, which launched the Civil Disobedience Movement, began in which year?", ["1919", "1920", "1942", "1930"], 3, "The Salt March started from Sabarmati Ashram on 12 March 1930 and reached Dandi in April. 1920 marks Non-Cooperation and 1942 the Quit India Movement."], ["Language Comprehension", "The firm hired temporary staff to augment its sales force before the festive season.\nWhich word is the antonym of AUGMENT?", ["Enlarge", "Supplement", "Diminish", "Amplify"], 2, "To augment is to increase or add to something. Diminish means to reduce, so it is the antonym; the other three are near-synonyms of augment."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "Pipe X fills a tank in 20 minutes and pipe Y in 30 minutes, while outlet Z empties the full tank in 15 minutes. If all three are opened together on an empty tank, how long will it take to fill it?", ["60 minutes", "12 minutes", "36 minutes", "45 minutes"], 0, "Net rate = 1/20 + 1/30 − 1/15 = (3 + 2 − 4)/60 = 1/60 of the tank per minute, so the tank fills in 60 minutes."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "What is the difference between the compound interest (compounded annually) and the simple interest on ₹12,000 for 2 years at 10% per annum?", ["₹100", "₹240", "₹120", "₹1,200"], 2, "For 2 years, CI − SI = P(r/100)² = 12,000 × 0.01 = ₹120. The extra amount is simply interest earned in year two on the first year's interest of ₹1,200."], ["Logical Reasoning", "Pointing to a young man, Riya says, \"He is the son of the only son of my grandfather.\" Assuming Riya's grandfather is her paternal grandfather, how is the man related to Riya?", ["Brother", "Cousin", "Uncle", "Nephew"], 0, "The only son of Riya's paternal grandfather is her father. The son of her father is her brother. A cousin would need the grandfather to have another child."], ["Logical Reasoning", "In a class of 45 students, Mohan ranks 12th from the top. What is his rank from the bottom?", ["33rd", "35th", "34th", "32nd"], 2, "Rank from bottom = total − rank from top + 1 = 45 − 12 + 1 = 34. Forgetting to add 1 gives 33rd, a common slip."], ["Quantitative Techniques & Data Interpretation", "If A : B = 3 : 4 and B : C = 6 : 7, what is A : B : C?", ["3 : 4 : 7", "18 : 24 : 21", "9 : 12 : 14", "9 : 12 : 16"], 2, "Make B common: 3:4 becomes 9:12 and 6:7 becomes 12:14. Hence A : B : C = 9 : 12 : 14. Simply joining 3:4 and 7 ignores the different values of B."], ["Language Comprehension", "Fill in the blank with the most appropriate word.\nDespite repeated reminders, the vendor remained ______ about the delivery date and gave no clear answer.", ["punctual", "transparent", "evasive", "grateful"], 2, "'Despite repeated reminders' and 'gave no clear answer' signal avoidance, so evasive fits. Transparent would mean the opposite, being open and clear."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "In the Lean Startup approach, a Minimum Viable Product (MVP) is best described as:", ["the cheapest product a firm can sell at a profit in the mass market", "a fully featured product launched only after detailed market research", "a prototype that is shown only to investors before any funding round", "the simplest version of a product that lets the team learn from real customers"], 3, "Eric Ries defines the MVP as the version that allows maximum validated learning about customers with the least effort. Its purpose is learning, not a polished or cheapest launch."], ["Innovation & Entrepreneurship", "A food-delivery startup finds that offices, not households, are its most loyal customers, and reshapes its model to serve corporate canteens. In startup terminology, this is a:", ["Spin-off", "Merger", "Pivot", "Bootstrap"], 2, "A pivot is a structured change of strategy to test a new hypothesis while keeping what has been learned. A spin-off creates a separate company; bootstrapping concerns self-funding."]]};
